 The cross product can be found using the determinant of a matrix with the unit vectors $\hat{i}$, $\hat{j}$, and $\hat{k}$ in the first row, the components of the first vector in the second row, and the components of the second vector in the third row. Specifically, the cross product $\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}$ is given by: \[
\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \begin{vmatrix} \hat{i} & \hat{j} & \hat{k} \\ a_1 & a_2 & a_3 \\ b_1 & b_2 & b_3 \end{vmatrix}
\] where $\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} a_1 \\ a_2 \\ a_3 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \\ b_3 \end{pmatrix}$. Here, $\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 4 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix}$. Therefore, the cross product is given by: \[
\begin{vmatrix} \hat{i} & \hat{j} & \hat{k} \\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 & -5 \end{vmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} (-1)(-5) - (2)(4) \\ (2)(3) - (1)(-5) \\ (1)(4) - (-1)(3) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 11 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}
\] </think> <answer> $\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 11 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}$
